WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.840
October 2009 was the worst month
of the worst year of the Great Recession.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.280
One out of every ten Americans
was out of work.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.200
It was bad.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.300
But not as bad as the worst year
of the Great Depression,

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.340
when the unemployment rate
was one in four.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.280
Few Americans alive today have ever seen that
many people out of work.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:23.340
Until now.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.000
"Coronavirus outbreaks at meatpacking plants,
forcing many to close."

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.560
"Ford, General Motors, and Fiat Chrysler,

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.500
all temporarily closing US plants
because of the coronavirus."

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.900
"Parking lots, bare.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.840
Retail stores, corporate offices,
all closed."

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.500
By the end of April 2020, 30 million Americans
had filed unemployment claims.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.620
Economists estimated that the
US unemployment rate was about 13%.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.660
The highest since the Great Depression.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.079
But in some other countries,

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:54.320
like the UK, for instance, it’s a totally
different story.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.380
Factories, restaurants,
all that stuff is closed.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:58.300
But this?

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.140
This isn’t happening.

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:04.660
In the US, lawmakers have assumed that
all those closed factories, shops, and restaurants

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.380
have one inevitable outcome:
mass unemployment.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.070
But what if that’s wrong?

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.280
What if millions of people didn’t have to
lose their jobs?

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.720
What if it didn’t have to be this way?

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.860
For most of US history, if you were laid off,
you didn’t have a lot of options.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.750
Churches and charities did what they could,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.180
but for the most part, you were on your own.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.990
That changed during the Great Depression.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.420
With help from the federal government,

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.620
states began to hold back a share
of every worker’s paycheck.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.280
That money went into a fund that workers
could tap into if they got laid off.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.860
"Workers in every state in the union are now protected if they are temporarily laid off or lose their jobs."

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:50.260
The system worked pretty well, as long as too many people didn’t lose their job all at once.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.720
But that is definitely not what happened during
the coronavirus lockdowns.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.020
You can think of the economy as a web of connections.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:12.560
During normal times, every day, billions of
dollars change hands across those connections,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.970
between different companies and industries.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.760
Airlines pay oil companies for jet fuel.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.120
Those oil companies pay computer engineers

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.580
to make software that helps them
find new reserves.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.800
And those software companies pay ad agencies
to make commercials for them.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.000
Then they pay to put those ads in front of
things you like to watch.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.610
And a tiny portion of that money helps pay
for us to make videos.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.500
We spend some of that money on, say, plane
tickets for reporting trips,

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.760
and the whole cycle repeats.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.360
During normal times, these connections are
what allow businesses to pay their employees.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.360
If some connections break,
and a business lays off workers,

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.060
unemployment insurance is there
to help them get by,

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:56.020
until those connections re-form,
and businesses are ready to hire again.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:59.220
But when the lockdowns started,
and businesses closed down,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.280
lots of those connections broke away entirely.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.980
Businesses laid off millions of workers
in just a few weeks,

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.260
faster than at any time in US history.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.680
In response, Congress
has passed several bills,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.520
aimed at helping states get unemployment benefits
to more people, more quickly.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.830
But even if that helps in the short term,
it might not be enough down the road.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.600
Because once the lockdowns are over,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.900
many of those businesses
simply won’t be there anymore.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:37.110
Even if they lay off employees,
businesses still have to pay rent.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.170
Plus insurance, utilities,
and other business costs.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:41.860
But there’s no money coming in.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.140
Once the lockdown is lifted,
and it’s safe to work again,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.980
a lot of businesses will be gone.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.680
And there will be way fewer jobs
to come back to.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.680
Lots of unemployed people
will likely stay unemployed.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.160
Which will draw the economic crisis
out even further.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.640
But things don’t have to turn out that way.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.239
Just like the US, most of the UK is on lockdown.

00:04:06.239 --> 00:04:09.500
Many of those connections
between businesses have fallen off.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.500
But instead of waiting for workers to get laid off,
the UK is doing something different.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.940
The way that they are going about it,
is saying to companies:

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.700
“We will pay you
to pay your employees."

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.300
Workers get paid 80% of their previous salary,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.500
and businesses get help
covering rent and other costs.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Denmark and the Netherlands have
put similar systems in place.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.000
In all of these countries, government support has
put the economy on pause,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.140
to keep it from falling apart later.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.320
In the US, Congress did set aside a chunk
of money for grants and loans to small businesses,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.860
in the hopes that they would
keep their workers on payroll.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.620
But to get the money, business owners had to apply
through commercial banks,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.520
like Chase and Bank of America.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:59.720
That extra step, combined with the onslaught
of applications, resulted in massive delays.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.100
By the time many small businesses got approval,
the fund was already empty.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.340
When the US first set up unemployment insurance
during the Great Depression,

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.400
that idea didn’t come out of thin air.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.600
Policymakers studied similar systems in England
and Germany, and then adapted them.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.540
This pandemic transcends national borders.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.540
The solutions should too.

